## Performance Modes

### 1. General Purpose Mode (Default, Recommended)
- **Best for:** Most file serving workloads
- **Characteristics:**
  - Lowest latencies
  - Suitable for web serving, content management, general file serving
  - Recommended for 99% of use cases
- **Use when:** Performance is critical for general-purpose workloads

```bash
# Create EFS with General Purpose mode (default)
aws efs create-file-system \
  --performance-mode generalPurpose \
  --throughput-mode bursting \
  --encrypted \
  --tags Key=Name,Value=MyEFS
```

### 2. Max IO Performance Mode
- **Best for:** Highly parallelized workloads
- **Characteristics:**
  - Higher levels of aggregate throughput and operations per second
  - Trade-off: Slightly higher latencies
  - Suitable for big data analytics, media processing, genomic analysis
- **Use when:** Throughput and operations-per-second matter more than latency

```bash
# Create EFS with Max IO mode
aws efs create-file-system \
  --performance-mode maxIO \
  --throughput-mode bursting \
  --encrypted \
  --tags Key=Name,Value=HighThroughputEFS
```

**Performance Comparison:**

| Aspect | General Purpose | Max IO |
|--------|-----------------|--------|
| Latency | Lower (ms) | Higher (ms) |
| Throughput | Good | Higher |
| Operations/sec | Good | Higher |
| Best for | Web, CMS, general | Big data, analytics |

