## Throughput Modes

### 1. Bursting Throughput Mode (Default)
- **Baseline:** 50 MB/s per TB of data stored
- **Burst:** Up to 100 MB/s per TB (30 minutes per day per GB)
- **Cost:** Lower (based on storage only)
- **Best for:** Typical file serving workloads with occasional spikes

```bash
# Create EFS with Bursting throughput
aws efs create-file-system \
  --throughput-mode bursting \
  --encrypted \
  --tags Key=Name,Value=BurstingEFS
```

**Example Calculation:**
```
File system size: 100 GB
Baseline throughput: 50 MB/s per TB = 50 × 0.1 = 5 MB/s
Burst throughput: 100 MB/s per TB = 100 × 0.1 = 10 MB/s

Can burst up to 10 MB/s for 30 minutes × 100 = 3,000 minutes = 50 hours per day
```

### 2. Provisioned Throughput Mode
- **Fixed throughput:** You specify desired throughput (1 MB/s to 1,024 MB/s)
- **Cost:** Higher (based on provisioned throughput)
- **Best for:** Consistent, predictable throughput requirements

```bash
# Create EFS with Provisioned throughput (100 MB/s)
aws efs create-file-system \
  --throughput-mode provisioned \
  --provisioned-throughput-in-mibps 100 \
  --encrypted \
  --tags Key=Name,Value=ProvisionedEFS
```

**Throughput Mode Comparison:**

| Aspect | Bursting | Provisioned |
|--------|----------|-------------|
| Cost Model | Storage-based | Throughput-based |
| Baseline | 50 MB/s per TB | User-defined |
| Burst Capability | 100 MB/s per TB | Yes (spillover) |
| Best for | Typical workloads | Consistent high throughput |
| Price per TB | Lower | Higher |

