## Step-by-Step Examples

### Example 1: Create EFS and Mount on EC2 Instances

#### Step 1: Create Security Group for EFS

```bash
# Create security group
aws ec2 create-security-group \
  --group-name efs-sg \
  --description "Security group for EFS mount targets" \
  --vpc-id vpc-12345678

# Get security group ID
SG_ID=$(aws ec2 describe-security-groups \
  --filters Name=group-name,Values=efs-sg \
  --query 'SecurityGroups[0].GroupId' \
  --output text)

# Allow NFS traffic (port 2049) from EC2 instances
aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \
  --group-id $SG_ID \
  --protocol tcp \
  --port 2049 \
  --source-security-group-id $SG_ID

# Allow traffic from EC2 security group (optional additional security group)
aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \
  --group-id $SG_ID \
  --protocol tcp \
  --port 2049 \
  --source-security-group-id sg-ec2instances
```

#### Step 2: Create EFS File System

```bash
# Create EFS file system
aws efs create-file-system \
  --performance-mode generalPurpose \
  --throughput-mode bursting \
  --encrypted \
  --tags Key=Name,Value=SharedData Key=Environment,Value=Production

# Get File System ID
FILE_SYSTEM_ID=$(aws efs describe-file-systems \
  --query 'FileSystems[0].FileSystemId' \
  --output text)

echo "File System ID: $FILE_SYSTEM_ID"
```

**Output:**
```
{
  "FileSystemId": "fs-1234567890abcdef0",
  "CreationTime": "2025-11-25T17:00:00Z",
  "FileSystemStatus": "creating",
  "PerformanceMode": "generalPurpose",
  "ThroughputMode": "bursting",
  "Encrypted": true,
  "Size": {
    "Value": 0,
    "Timestamp": "2025-11-25T17:00:00Z"
  },
  "Tags": [
    {
      "Key": "Name",
      "Value": "SharedData"
    }
  ]
}
```

#### Step 3: Create Mount Targets in Each Availability Zone

```bash
# Get VPC subnets in different AZs
SUBNET_1=$(aws ec2 describe-subnets \
  --filters Name=vpc-id,Values=vpc-12345678 Name=availability-zone,Values=us-east-1a \
  --query 'Subnets[0].SubnetId' \
  --output text)

SUBNET_2=$(aws ec2 describe-subnets \
  --filters Name=vpc-id,Values=vpc-12345678 Name=availability-zone,Values=us-east-1b \
  --query 'Subnets[0].SubnetId' \
  --output text)

# Create mount target in us-east-1a
aws efs create-mount-target \
  --file-system-id $FILE_SYSTEM_ID \
  --subnet-id $SUBNET_1 \
  --security-groups $SG_ID

# Create mount target in us-east-1b
aws efs create-mount-target \
  --file-system-id $FILE_SYSTEM_ID \
  --subnet-id $SUBNET_2 \
  --security-groups $SG_ID

echo "Mount targets created"
```

#### Step 4: Install EFS Utils on EC2 Instance

SSH to EC2 instance and install utilities:

```bash
# For Amazon Linux 2
sudo yum install -y nfs-utils
sudo yum install -y amazon-efs-utils

# For Ubuntu
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y nfs-common
sudo apt-get install -y amazon-efs-utils

# For RHEL/CentOS
sudo yum install -y nfs-utils
sudo yum install amazon-efs-utils -y
```

#### Step 5: Create Mount Point and Mount EFS

```bash
# Create mount point
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/efs

# Mount EFS using mount helper (recommended for encryption)
sudo mount -t efs -o tls $FILE_SYSTEM_ID:/ /mnt/efs

# Alternative: Mount using NFS directly (without encryption)
# sudo mount -t nfs4 -o nfsvers=4.1,rsize=1048576,wsize=1048576,hard,timeo=600,retrans=2 \
#   fs-12345678.efs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com:/ /mnt/efs

# Verify mount
df -h
# Output:
# Filesystem                                    Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on
# fs-1234567890abcdef0.efs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com:/  1.0P     0  1.0P   0% /mnt/efs
```

#### Step 6: Persist Mount in /etc/fstab

```bash
# Get file system DNS name
FILE_SYSTEM_DNS=$(aws efs describe-file-systems \
  --file-system-id $FILE_SYSTEM_ID \
  --query 'FileSystems[0].Name' \
  --output text)

# Add to /etc/fstab
echo "$FILE_SYSTEM_ID:/ /mnt/efs efs defaults,_netdev,tls 0 0" | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab

# Or using mount helper
# echo "$FILE_SYSTEM_ID:/ /mnt/efs efs defaults,_netdev 0 0" | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab
```

#### Step 7: Test Write and Read

```bash
# Create test file
echo "Hello from EFS!" | sudo tee /mnt/efs/test.txt

# Verify from another instance (mount EFS there too, then read):
sudo cat /mnt/efs/test.txt
# Output: Hello from EFS!

# Create shared directory
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/efs/shared-data
sudo chmod 777 /mnt/efs/shared-data

# Create test file accessible from other instances
echo "Shared by Instance 1" | sudo tee /mnt/efs/shared-data/instance1.txt
```

#### Step 8: Mount Same EFS on Second EC2 Instance

Repeat Steps 4-6 on second EC2 instance in different AZ:

```bash
# Install utilities
sudo yum install -y nfs-utils amazon-efs-utils

# Create mount point
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/efs

# Mount EFS (same FILE_SYSTEM_ID)
sudo mount -t efs -o tls fs-1234567890abcdef0:/ /mnt/efs

# Verify shared files
ls -la /mnt/efs/shared-data/
# Output shows files created by Instance 1

cat /mnt/efs/shared-data/instance1.txt
# Output: Shared by Instance 1
```

