### Example 2: Use Access Points for Application-Level Permissions

#### Create Access Point

```bash
# Create access point for application
aws efs create-access-point \
  --file-system-id fs-1234567890abcdef0 \
  --posix-user Uid=1000,Gid=1000 \
  --root-directory Path="/app-data",CreationInfo=OwnerUid=1000,OwnerGid=1000,Permissions=755 \
  --tags Key=Name,Value=AppAccessPoint

# Get Access Point ID
ACCESS_POINT_ID=$(aws efs describe-access-points \
  --file-system-id fs-1234567890abcdef0 \
  --query 'AccessPoints[0].AccessPointId' \
  --output text)

echo "Access Point ID: $ACCESS_POINT_ID"
```

#### Mount Using Access Point

```bash
# Create application mount point
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/efs-app

# Mount using access point
sudo mount -t efs -o tls,accesspoint=$ACCESS_POINT_ID fs-1234567890abcdef0:/ /mnt/efs-app

# Verify - should show /app-data as root
df -h /mnt/efs-app
ls -la /mnt/efs-app
```

**Benefits of Access Points:**
- Enforces user identity and permissions
- Applications run with specific UID/GID
- Enforces directory-level permissions
- Simplifies application deployment

