## Real-World Use Cases

### Use Case 1: Shared Web Application Content

**Scenario:** Multiple EC2 instances serving web content from shared file system

```bash
# Create EFS for web content
aws efs create-file-system \
  --performance-mode generalPurpose \
  --throughput-mode bursting \
  --encrypted \
  --tags Key=Name,Value=WebContent Key=Application,Value=WebApp

# Create access point for web server
aws efs create-access-point \
  --file-system-id fs-web12345 \
  --posix-user Uid=33,Gid=33 \
  --root-directory Path="/var/www/html",CreationInfo=OwnerUid=33,OwnerGid=33,Permissions=755 \
  --tags Key=Name,Value=WebAccessPoint

# On each web server instance:
sudo mount -t efs -o tls,accesspoint=fsap-web12345 fs-web12345:/ /var/www/html

# All instances now serve same content
# Deploy changes once, propagate to all instances
```

**Benefits:**
- Single deployment location for all web servers
- Automatic content replication across instances
- No need for file sync tools
- Horizontal scaling easier

### Use Case 2: Machine Learning Model Training

**Scenario:** Multiple GPU instances reading training data from shared EFS

```bash
# Create EFS for training data
aws efs create-file-system \
  --performance-mode maxIO \
  --throughput-mode provisioned \
  --provisioned-throughput-in-mibps 500 \
  --encrypted \
  --tags Key=Name,Value=MLTrainingData Key=Workload,Value=DataScience

# Create mount targets in all AZs with GPU instances
# Mount on each GPU instance:
sudo mount -t efs -o tls fs-ml12345:/ /mnt/ml-data

# Training script reads from /mnt/ml-data
# Multiple instances train on same dataset simultaneously
```

**Benefits:**
- Thousands of EC2 instances can read same data
- No local storage bottleneck
- Scales automatically with demand
- Max IO mode handles high concurrency

### Use Case 3: Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD)

**Scenario:** Build artifacts and logs shared across CI/CD pipeline

```bash
# Create EFS for CI/CD artifacts
aws efs create-file-system \
  --performance-mode generalPurpose \
  --throughput-mode bursting \
  --encrypted \
  --tags Key=Name,Value=CICDArtefacts Key=Pipeline,Value=Build

# Jenkins/GitLab CI mount EFS
# Build artifacts stored in /mnt/efs/artifacts
# Logs stored in /mnt/efs/logs

# Structure:
# /mnt/efs/
#   ├── artifacts/
#   │   ├── build-1/
#   │   ├── build-2/
#   │   └── build-3/
#   ├── logs/
#   │   ├── build-1.log
#   │   ├── build-2.log
#   │   └── build-3.log
#   └── cache/
#       └── dependencies/
```

**Benefits:**
- Shared build cache across all build agents
- Centralized artifact storage
- Easy access to historical logs
- No S3 sync overhead

### Use Case 4: Database Backup and Recovery

**Scenario:** Multiple database servers backing up to shared EFS

```bash
# Create EFS for database backups
aws efs create-file-system \
  --performance-mode generalPurpose \
  --throughput-mode provisioned \
  --provisioned-throughput-in-mibps 100 \
  --storage-redundancy-type STANDARD \
  --encrypted \
  --tags Key=Name,Value=DBBackups Key=DataType,Value=Critical

# Backup script on each database:
#!/bin/bash
DB_NAME="mydb"
BACKUP_PATH="/mnt/efs/backups/${DB_NAME}"
TIMESTAMP=$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)

mysqldump -u root -p${DB_PASSWORD} ${DB_NAME} | gzip > ${BACKUP_PATH}/backup_${TIMESTAMP}.sql.gz

# Retention policy: Keep last 30 days
find ${BACKUP_PATH} -type f -mtime +30 -delete

echo "Backup completed: ${BACKUP_PATH}/backup_${TIMESTAMP}.sql.gz"
```

**Benefits:**
- Centralized backup location
- Easy recovery from any database instance
- Cost-effective vs S3 for frequent access
- Automatic scaling with data growth

