## Best Practices

### 1. Use Security Groups Properly
```bash
# Allow NFS traffic only from authorized EC2 instances
aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \
  --group-id sg-efs1234 \
  --protocol tcp \
  --port 2049 \
  --source-security-group-id sg-ec2instances

# Don't allow from 0.0.0.0/0 (security risk)
```

### 2. Enable Encryption
```bash
# Always enable encryption at rest
aws efs create-file-system \
  --encrypted \
  --kms-key-id alias/aws/efs
```

### 3. Use Mount Targets in Multiple AZs
```bash
# Create mount targets in each AZ for high availability
# Prevents single point of failure
# Instances mount from nearest mount target
```

### 4. Implement Access Points for Applications
```bash
# Enforce user identity and permissions per application
aws efs create-access-point \
  --file-system-id fs-12345 \
  --posix-user Uid=1000,Gid=1000 \
  --root-directory Path="/app"
```

### 5. Enable Lifecycle Policies
```bash
# Transition infrequently accessed files to IA
aws efs put-lifecycle-configuration \
  --file-system-id fs-12345 \
  --lifecycle-policies TransitionToIA=AFTER_30_DAYS
```

### 6. Monitor Performance
```bash
# Set CloudWatch alarms for burst credit balance
aws cloudwatch put-metric-alarm \
  --alarm-name efs-low-burst-credits \
  --alarm-description "Alert when burst credits low" \
  --metric-name BurstCreditBalance \
  --namespace AWS/EFS \
  --statistic Average \
  --period 300 \
  --threshold 10737418240 \
  --comparison-operator LessThanThreshold
```

### 7. Tag Resources for Cost Management
```bash
aws efs create-tags \
  --file-system-id fs-12345 \
  --tags Key=Environment,Value=Production \
         Key=CostCenter,Value=Engineering \
         Key=Application,Value=WebApp
```

### 8. Regular Backups
```bash
# Backup critical data periodically
aws efs create-backup-vault --backup-vault-name efs-backups

# Or use AWS Backup service for automated backups
```

